#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "6010c0f3-67d3-5b21-af26-695a864fb42a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3541" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ < \\phi < 90^\\circ$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $\\phi$?" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 2; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= 1 \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (1) \\\\\n&= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "91e6008e-5c37-55aa-8cf6-cac61198f459" :question/math-academy-id "e-3542" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R$?" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x + ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ + \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x + R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = R\\sin(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = \\sqrt{2}; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= \\sqrt{2}^{2} + \\sqrt{7}^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 2 + 7 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nR &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "557b1b6e-c54a-5fa9-85e1-3cea346c3bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3543" :question/problem "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi)\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.588rad\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= (3)^{2} + (2)^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 4 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 13 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "9ea36397-ab9d-5567-8b38-3e83ba624e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3544" :question/problem "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\cos (x + ϕ) &= R(\\cos x\\cos ϕ - \\sin x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\cos x - R\\sin ϕ\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -(R \\sin \\phi)\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\sin ϕ)^{2} + (R\\cos ϕ)^{2} &= 3^{2} + (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\sin^{2} ϕ + \\cos^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 3 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 12 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21967a74-a70c-5d80-9061-e2f8238e3b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266559} {:answer/id #uuid "ce34e396-33a4-584c-9f1d-8f7056a38a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266560} {:answer/id #uuid "623ea025-1e92-59f8-926d-0d25222e7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266561} {:answer/id #uuid "ec117651-6ebe-569e-b39f-3266d56d5381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a963b7b-2737-594d-9e91-6c643c1b46ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce34e396-33a4-584c-9f1d-8f7056a38a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266560} :answer-field/id #uuid "c858464a-8f09-513f-bda6-85d1356abf8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3afc8de-0496-5d66-b501-f8daf7600278" :question/math-academy-id "q-107565" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "959266a1-b43b-5ccf-85f5-33feb2ee6293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266573} {:answer/id #uuid "ca59d549-212d-5afa-a3a0-be266414dd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266574} {:answer/id #uuid "3415ec0b-63df-5359-ada7-30638d1b4f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+0.464)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266575} {:answer/id #uuid "10598812-0f6a-5d50-bf5a-847740aef2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.351)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266576} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f504f-5d44-5838-a29e-902c6f03b066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca59d549-212d-5afa-a3a0-be266414dd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266574} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8723bed-db8d-58c9-bc0f-1d4124803bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db0aa66b-7e8e-5027-b840-3d5fc4d9828f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107577" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =2 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & =1.107\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4\\cdot 5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895a52d3-901d-5a3d-bcf3-e59e1972e8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093706} {:answer/id #uuid "ab538622-6dee-5a95-8eff-75ccb9d525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093707} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc19cb4-22f9-59f6-b0cf-3bd2143495fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093708} {:answer/id #uuid "7786a5b9-7afc-5184-a6ed-3163876fc05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093709} {:answer/id #uuid "77b87860-d1c7-5c53-92fd-e7f7daaf0d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab538622-6dee-5a95-8eff-75ccb9d525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093707} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9b2d63-d5fe-5355-b91d-cce5cca7c1c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093705}] :question/id #uuid "60acf08a-2dba-5856-a17a-f9ec2a8623fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-11296" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\sin x + 3\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin (x + ϕ)$ with $R > 0$, what is the value of $R$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7b17ec-8aaa-5772-a0fc-5d2c5bee28d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7b17ec-8aaa-5772-a0fc-5d2c5bee28d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} :answer-field/id #uuid "04c55e61-c577-55c9-90b3-2c61887c4e82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cec72bc3-9ce9-56a6-be97-f8888cf3eb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-274609" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdba17c-ddca-5813-8832-901ff88a1adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095173} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d673d2-953b-5915-b717-3f11104a41ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095174} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d718c-4e0f-5d1e-bbd8-a9618cfe962e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095175} {:answer/id #uuid "4da046b0-6db0-5896-8e56-abfc95c537b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095176} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf2366f-8017-58c1-b288-50532ba2f0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d718c-4e0f-5d1e-bbd8-a9618cfe962e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095175} :answer-field/id #uuid "68c19438-0750-5202-8c3d-71bea293c478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095172}] :question/id #uuid "d31aaa3c-4e44-57d5-8b17-880f83f68511" :question/math-academy-id "q-30580" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$\\sin x + \\sqrt{3}\\cos x =$" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055b7f47-2047-59ac-ab6b-db10bd1d99bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266604} {:answer/id #uuid "1b05f1ba-cbdb-5c22-ae61-26fd8698cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80862d-ceed-5218-a10a-5fa7310fcca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266606} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae4900d-1807-5425-9844-93cf3e86d8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266607} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b90bb-0591-5edb-8707-f322beaccd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b05f1ba-cbdb-5c22-ae61-26fd8698cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fa1131c-da92-5cd8-8b0a-6ac1d605eea1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fd10193-69cb-5212-ac5c-9e4d5831b0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30588" :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74765624-2997-556c-bb1d-96da05bb7da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266611} {:answer/id #uuid "b22eb970-03fb-5ee4-b8e1-f0ace298ff5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74765624-2997-556c-bb1d-96da05bb7da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266611} :answer-field/id #uuid "1003564f-4545-5b97-90dc-4d5adaced41e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50f75dc0-69cb-5287-84d4-979b01969621" :question/math-academy-id "q-327669" :question/problem "Given that the expression $7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=7 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={7}^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =49+72 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =121 \\\\ R & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d51fa9-e55e-56a6-b3c7-3bfdcb6468f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266622} {:answer/id #uuid "34cd456b-03d7-5b40-b2d4-4bf35537c125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d51fa9-e55e-56a6-b3c7-3bfdcb6468f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4a84f0-1887-5f8a-8f60-ebafa1362b37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4162ecbe-b322-51b0-80d6-aa2e8ed16fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-327979" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0,$ what is the **exact** value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ \n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2726a2e-489b-51dd-990c-aefd7189dd43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276020} {:answer/id #uuid "b70b4a52-6461-5642-a5fe-aee5e2e51693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276021} {:answer/id #uuid "9969be14-ed57-529f-b3f1-c4d6b54604ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276022} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a286e4-c84a-58de-b270-c54eddb95ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276023} {:answer/id #uuid "8250ef2f-7289-5956-b472-73327e0a40bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9969be14-ed57-529f-b3f1-c4d6b54604ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276022} :answer-field/id #uuid "98df750c-d872-5b24-ad78-2b70b57bf185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1233738d-9980-5519-b543-2a6375658840" :question/math-academy-id "q-65574" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df9cb234-eed4-507c-9ec1-9e9aceafae02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276027} {:answer/id #uuid "c6843a56-169c-5c75-8868-e9c86cab9108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276028} {:answer/id #uuid "82f6df96-b111-5419-bd2a-259f3d9437cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276029} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfff3f8-100c-55a5-a281-9de36806e295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276030} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dbb64a-3cac-5b76-afcb-707ed66a1feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cfff3f8-100c-55a5-a281-9de36806e295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276030} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed077ed-e31f-5fb4-aff3-0324418e805a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2482eaaf-f514-5577-a783-2f1ca414760f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80135" :question/problem "Given that the expression $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe968fbe-d97d-504c-b62e-527278272744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281895} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f0e66f-9dfb-5d78-b659-a4fae80d7f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-1.156)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281896} {:answer/id #uuid "c74cb847-af86-546c-a94c-daee7590d07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.854)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281897} {:answer/id #uuid "5df9f194-a06d-5fe6-a71d-aa94e6598893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.916)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281898} {:answer/id #uuid "79bca67a-6243-5257-b2e0-cd8c682f2588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-1.054)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe968fbe-d97d-504c-b62e-527278272744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281895} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3a0c80-1834-5558-a1e9-38a21bc6e1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da754d54-7ad0-5021-82bb-f04c6fa332b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80147" :question/problem "$2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)≈$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.955\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =8+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =24 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)\n$$"}]}
